  "result": "`click.KeyValue` is in place, and `@click.option('--set', type=click.KeyValue())` now turns `key=value` strings into `(key, value)` tuples. The full test suite passes (1914 passed, 102 skipped), including the new tests. I couldn't run the type checkers because neither `mypy` nor `pyright` is installed here.\n\nHow it parses:\n- **Valid input:** the string is split at the first `=`, so `a=b=c` gives `(\"a\", \"b=c\")`. An empty value is allowed, so `a=` gives `(\"a\", \"\")`.\n- **Invalid input:** a string with no `=` (like `abc`), an empty key (like `=value`) or an empty string raises an error saying `'abc' is not a valid key=value pair.` From the command line that ends with exit code 2.\n- **Already a tuple:** a value that has already been converted is returned unchanged, as click requires of its types.\n\nThe new class is in `src/click/types.py`, right after `Tuple`. I also exported it from `src/click/__init__.py`, added it to `docs/api.md` and `docs/parameter-types.md`, and added an entry to `CHANGES.md`. The tests are at the end of `tests/test_types.py`: direct conversion cases, the three failure cases, and a command-line test using `--set` several times.\n\nNothing is committed yet.",
